.lua
book.chapter(32, "Embedding MetaML in C++")
.endlua
.sh 2 "The host owns the session"
.pp
The C++ interpreter is header-only and requires C++20 through DSLtk.hpp.
Include MetaML.hpp, construct metaml::Session, load a chosen library tree,
and inspect every returned Result. A successful eval yields a metaml::Value;
a failed eval yields diagnostics. The host controls session lifetime,
source selection, output routing, primitive installation, and synchronization.
Embedding is therefore more than replacing a shell command with a function
call: it establishes a native ownership and capability boundary.
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "MetaML.hpp"
#include <iostream>
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    metaml::Session session;
    auto loaded = session.load_stdlib(argc > 1 ? argv[1] : "metalib");
    if (loaded.is_err()) {
        for (const auto& d : loaded.unwrap_err())
            std::cerr << session.render(d);
        return 1;
    }
    auto result = session.eval("List.sum [10, 20, 12]");
    if (result.is_err()) {
        for (const auto& d : result.unwrap_err())
            std::cerr << session.render(d);
        return 1;
    }
    std::cout << metaml::to_string(result.unwrap()) << '\n';
}]=], "A complete C++ embedding client")
book.code("sh", [=[c++ -std=c++20 -O0 -pthread -I. embedding.cpp -o embedding
./embedding metalib]=], "Compile the preceding listing from the DSLtk directory")
.endlua
.pp
The D counterpart appears in the next chapter and follows the same language
contract. C++ memory management is not a language-visible difference in
MetaML syntax. Avoid sharing native representation assumptions across the
dialect boundary; compare evaluated values, diagnostics, and AST behavior
instead. The optional C ABI is separately compiled but does not remove the
header-only property of this direct C++ interface.
.sh 2 "Register trusted native operations"
.pp
Typed register_primitive derives a language type from supported native
signatures. Integers, floating values, booleans, strings, optionals, vectors,
pairs, and selected Result types have conversion support. Registration
explicitly trusts the primitive and grants its exact name. Keep native
arithmetic safe: MetaML's wrapping rules do not make overflowing signed C++
arithmetic defined inside an arbitrary user callback.
.lua
book.code("cpp-fragment", [=[session.register_primitive("Host.describe", [](std::int64_t n) {
    return std::string("count=") + std::to_string(n);
});
auto answer = session.eval("Host.describe 42");]=], "Typed registration in an existing C++ session")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[session.register_primitive("Host.describe", (long n) {
    import std.conv : to;
    return "count=" ~ to!string(n);
});
auto answer = session.eval("Host.describe 42");]=], "The corresponding D registration")
.endlua
.pp
The low-level overload accepts a language type source, arity, and PrimFn.
It is appropriate for callbacks into MetaML, custom Value manipulation,
and opaque resources. The advertised type and arity are a promise the
implementation must honor. Validate native conversions and retain useful
primitive names in diagnostics rather than allowing accidental casts to
silently reinterpret data at the boundary.
.sh 2 "Install libraries without granting everything"
.pp
Metalib.h exposes install_all and smaller installation layers. Installers
establish declarations and primitive implementations but do not automatically
grant non-core capabilities. Configure an allowlist afterwards. Install before
loading the standard library when host modules introduce types or constructors
that source wrappers need. A developer CLI's permissive bundle is a useful
workbench default, not a required embedding policy.
.lua
book.code("cpp-fragment", [=[// Also include "metalib/cpp/Metalib.h".
metalib::install_all(session);
session.set_primitive_allowlist({"Hash.*", "Buffer.*"});
auto loaded = session.load_stdlib("metalib");]=], "Installation and permission are separate operations")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[// Also import metalib.d.Metalib.
installAll(session);
session.set_primitive_allowlist(["Hash.*", "Buffer.*"]);
auto loaded = session.load_stdlib("metalib");]=], "The corresponding D installation policy")
.endlua
.pp
Prefer exact names when a script needs only one operation, or module patterns
when the whole module is intentionally available. Core operations and source
imports have their own behavior, so an empty non-core allowlist is not a
complete isolation boundary. Chapter 34 explains how to combine a deliberate
runtime surface with process-level controls when the application requires
stronger limits on execution or external access.
.sh 2 "Retained values have lifetimes"
.pp
Global retrieves a bound value, and call applies a function Value from native
code. Keep the Session alive while using values associated with its closures,
primitives, and internal machinery. Re-registering a primitive preserves old
callable objects' lifetime inside the session, but does not make those values
independent of the session. A native cache of functions therefore needs an
explicit owner, invalidation policy, and synchronization strategy.
.pp
Do not evaluate one Session concurrently from unrelated threads. Handle-local
locks in buffers or tables do not serialize the type environment, module
initialization, or evaluator state. A straightforward host design gives each
worker its own session or funnels requests through one owner. Output callbacks
should avoid re-entering that owner synchronously, especially while the
runtime's output mutex is held.
.sh 2 "Preserve structured errors"
.pp
Recoverable standard exceptions thrown by invoked native primitives become
M0409 diagnostics. Language errors retain their language codes. This lets a
host present an ordinary callback failure without exposing internal exception
types to scripts, but it cannot recover from undefined behavior or native
process faults. Check eval, load, and call results before unwrapping and
retain source text long enough to render useful diagnostics.
.pp
For service integration, separate returned values from output bytes and
diagnostics. A script can print before ultimately failing, so success status
alone does not describe everything that happened. A host may buffer output
until completion for presentation, but that is an application policy rather
than interpreter rollback. Document whether partially produced output is
discarded, published, or attached to the error report.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read Session, primitive registration templates, PrimContext, and Value in
MetaML.hpp, then inspect metalib/cpp/Metalib.h. Compile the complete client
and add a trusted string-producing primitive with boundary-value tests.
Next retain a function returned by global and call it while its Session
remains alive. Draw the lifetime relationship explicitly before putting
that function into an asynchronous host task or cache.
